<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gansu</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gansu is a province of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, stretching from the Loess Plateau along the narrow Hexi (Gansu) Corridor towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lanzhou">Lanzhou</a> as its capital. Its eastern valleys were the early homeland of the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>, and the corridor carried the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> between China and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. Over the centuries it was contested by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetans">Tibetans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanguts">Tanguts</a>, and many short-lived states rose there during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixteen-kingdoms">Sixteen Kingdoms</a> period.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gansu.jpg" alt="Gansu" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Terry Wu · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hoodoos_at_Zhangye_Danxia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>According to the Shiji, Feizi, said to descend from the legendary adviser Gao Yao, was granted the settlement of Qin, in modern Qingshui County, <b>Gansu</b>, in the 9th century BCE, and in 897 BCE the area became a dependency for raising horses.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>According to the Shiji of Sima Qian, the Ying clan descended from Feizi, a skilled horse breeder whom King Xiao of Zhou rewarded with a small fief at Qin, in the upper Wei and Xihan river valleys of eastern <b>Gansu</b>; tradition dates the grant to about 897 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-qin">State of Qin · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Uyghurs who fled south founded the Ganzhou Uyghur Kingdom in modern <b>Gansu</b> and the Kingdom of Qocho near Turpan, where they turned to Buddhism and, in Mahmud al-Kashgari's words, were 'the strongest of the infidels'; Kashgari praised their Turkic as 'pure' and 'most correct'.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uyghur-khaganate">Uyghur Khaganate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Xianbei fought as mounted archers in the manner of the Xiongnu, and their strength lay in numbers, mobility and the ability to strike along the whole northern frontier of the Han from Liaodong to <b>Gansu</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/xianbei">Xianbei · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Modu's campaigns united the peoples of Mongolia and Inner Asia, destroyed and absorbed the Donghu confederacy to the east, defeated the Yuezhi of the <b>Gansu</b> region, and brought peoples further west under Xiongnu suzerainty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>When the Kyrgyz destroyed the khaganate in 840, the Uyghurs moved south and west, founding kingdoms in <b>Gansu</b> and in the oases of the Tarim Basin, above all the kingdom of Qocho near Turfan.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-turkic">Old Turkic · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li></ul></section>
